From f8d134452b7499766a5235f2c077347acf13604d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 18:50:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Publish a generated election as a challenge, with a withheld
 answer and a scorer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`docs/Challenge.md` is the statement, and it is short on purpose: a vague
one is won by redefinition.

**What is published.** Four files. The configuration, the ledger, the
release register -- and `supervision.json`, the office's own register,
which a real poll keeps behind its perimeter because absence from it is
conclusive proof of non-voting (§3.7). Handing it over is the whole
demonstration: an attacker gets the citizens' identifiers and the blinded
values `c` they asked the office to sign *together*, which is strictly
more than any observer of a real poll ever holds.

**The claim.** Exhibit a mapping from citizen identifiers to ad-hoc public
keys that is correct materially more often than guessing. It should hold
because the only object connecting the two is the blinding factor `r`,
which never left the voting application: `c = enc(k_p^a)·r^e` is uniformly
distributed and independent of `k_p^a`, so for every key in the ledger
there is an `r` making it the preimage of any given `c`. The hiding is
information-theoretic, which is why the register is publishable at all and
why the challenge is about the record rather than about a key length.

Four caveats in the statement, all load-bearing: the electorate is
synthetic (S8) while none of the cryptography is; ballots under one
ad-hoc key are linkable to each other by design, and that is variant B's
handle rather than a win; the dataset has no timestamps because the ledger
has none, so an attack assuming timing is out of scope here and in scope
for the design (A2); and the office could link if it retained `r` or the
issuance order, which it does not -- the two orders are drawn
independently and the registers are not even the same length.

**Two refusals for one mistake**, because publishing a real poll's
participation register cannot be taken back. `archive_at_close` writes the
register only when the configuration declares a generated electorate, and
`tools/publish_challenge.py` refuses a directory whose configuration does
not. Both read the flag from the configuration, so a reloaded election
cannot acquire the behaviour.

`supervision_register` moves out of the supervision app's closure to
module level, so the archive writes the bytes the origin serves. One
renderer: a challenge about a file written specially for the occasion is a
challenge about nothing. It counts as a supervisor download, because it is
one -- the archived file records its own release and says 1, not 0.

**The answer never leaves.** `publish_challenge.py` hashes the answer key
and never reads its contents; its SHA-256 goes in the manifest and in the
statement the bundle carries, before the challenge opens.
`test_the_answer_is_not_in_the_bundle` reads every byte published looking
for a key beside its citizen, rather than checking a file name.

**`tools/score_challenge.py` is the scoring rule as code**, because a rule
described and not implemented is a rule that gets argued about after
somebody claims a win. Exact binomial tail, no normal approximation --
the tail is the whole question and an approximation is worst exactly there
-- summed from the far end inward. Threshold 1e-3 rather than the
customary 0.05, because this is a public invitation and somebody will
submit many claims. A claim is scored over every identifier it names,
including ones the election has never heard of: counting only the
recognised ones would make padding free, and dropping them silently would
tell somebody working from a different election that they had merely
guessed badly. `--expect-digest` is the half of the commitment that
protects the claimant.

**The verifier says so, after its verdict rather than among the checks.** A
generated election passes every check -- that is the point of generating
one -- so the sentence a reader must carry away from a page of green ticks
is that passing them is not evidence anybody voted.

Four mutations, each dry-run caught by the test that names it: the
register archived for every election, the answer copied into the bundle,
a scorer that calls any hit a win, and a verifier that stops reporting
the electorate.

